    const Section(
      title: 'History & Systems Review:',
      content: 'Key areas to assess:\n- **Pain characterization**: Dermatomal distribution (C6 pattern: lateral arm, forearm, thumb, index finger). Quality (burning, shooting — radicular/neuropathic). Provocative factors (extension, ipsilateral rotation, Valsalva). Alleviating factors (abduction relief sign — resting hand on head reduces traction on nerve root).\n- **Neurologic symptoms**: Numbness, tingling, weakness — which specific muscles affected? Any bilateral symptoms or lower extremity symptoms that would suggest myelopathy?\n- **Red flags (myelopathy)**: Gait disturbance, bilateral hand clumsiness, bowel/bladder dysfunction, Lhermitte sign (electric sensation down spine with neck flexion). These change the diagnosis and urgency dramatically.\n- **Red flags (other)**: Fever, weight loss, night sweats (infection, malignancy). History of cancer. IV drug use. Recent trauma.\n- **Occupational history**: Prolonged computer use with poor ergonomics. Repetitive neck positions. This is highly relevant for both etiology and management.\n- **Prior cervical spine history**: Previous episodes, imaging, treatments, injections, or surgery.\n- **Psychosocial factors**: Work stress, sleep disruption, catastrophizing, fear-avoidance beliefs. These predict chronicity and should be addressed early.\n- **Medications**: NSAID use, acetaminophen, any prior opioid prescriptions, gabapentinoids, muscle relaxants tried.\n- **Functional impact**: Work productivity, driving, ADLs, exercise, sleep quality.',
