    const Section(
      title: '+ How do you localize this to a C6 radiculopathy, and when would you order imaging and electrodiagnostic studies?',
      content: '**Localization to C6 Nerve Root**:\n\nThe clinical picture is consistent with a **right C6 radiculopathy** based on the convergence of:\n\n| Finding | C6 Root |\n|---|---|\n| Pain distribution | Lateral arm, lateral forearm, thumb, index finger |\n| Sensory deficit | Lateral forearm, thumb |\n| Motor weakness | Biceps (elbow flexion), wrist extensors (extensor carpi radialis) |\n| Reflex diminished | Biceps, brachioradialis |\n| Provocative test | Spurling reproduces C6 dermatomal pain |\n\n**Comparison with Adjacent Levels**:\n- **C5**: Lateral arm/deltoid. Deltoid weakness. Biceps reflex. No hand symptoms.\n- **C6**: Lateral forearm, thumb, index finger. Biceps and wrist extensor weakness. Biceps/brachioradialis reflex.\n- **C7**: Posterior arm/forearm, middle finger. Triceps and wrist flexor weakness. Triceps reflex.\n- **C8**: Medial forearm, ring/small fingers. Grip and intrinsic hand weakness. No reliable reflex.\n\n**Most likely etiology**: C5-C6 disc herniation (most common level for cervical radiculopathy) or C5-C6 foraminal stenosis from spondylosis.\n\n**Imaging Approach**:\n1. **Cervical radiographs (AP, lateral, oblique)**: Reasonable initial study. May show disc space narrowing, foraminal narrowing on oblique views, osteophytes, loss of lordosis. Cannot visualize disc herniation or neural compression.\n2. **MRI cervical spine WITHOUT contrast**: Gold standard for soft tissue visualization. Indicated when:\n   - Neurologic deficit is present (this patient has weakness and reflex change — MRI is indicated)\n   - Red flags are present\n   - Symptoms persist beyond 4-6 weeks of conservative management\n   - Surgical intervention is being considered\n   - **For this patient**: MRI is indicated NOW given objective neurologic deficit (weakness, reflex change) at 6 weeks duration.\n3. **CT myelogram**: Alternative if MRI is contraindicated (pacemaker, severe claustrophobia). Superior for bony foraminal stenosis.\n\n**Electrodiagnostic Studies (EMG/NCS)**:\n- **Timing**: NOT useful in the first 2-3 weeks. Fibrillation potentials take 2-3 weeks to develop after axonal injury. Optimal timing is **3-6 weeks** after symptom onset. This patient at 6 weeks is in the ideal window.\n- **Indications**: Confirm radiculopathy, localize level, assess severity (axonal loss), rule out peripheral neuropathy or other diagnoses (brachial plexopathy, median neuropathy/CTS).\n- **Expected NCS findings**: Typically normal sensory studies (the lesion is proximal to the DRG, so peripheral sensory fibers remain intact). Motor amplitudes may be reduced if significant axonal loss. Normal distal latencies and conduction velocities.\n- **Expected Needle EMG findings**: \n  - Fibrillation potentials and positive sharp waves in C6-innervated muscles (biceps, brachioradialis, pronator teres, extensor carpi radialis) AND the cervical paraspinal muscles\n  - Reduced recruitment in affected muscles\n  - Large polyphasic MUPs if chronic reinnervation is occurring\n  - Normal findings in non-C6 muscles (deltoid — C5, triceps — C7) helps confirm the level\n  - **Key**: Paraspinal fibrillations localize the lesion to the root level rather than a more distal plexus or peripheral nerve lesion.',
