    const Section(
      title: '+ Outline your conservative management plan, indications for epidural injection, and surgical referral criteria.',
      content: '**Conservative Management (First-Line — resolves 80-90% of cases)**:\n\n**1. Pharmacologic Pain Management**:\n- **NSAIDs**: Continue meloxicam 15 mg daily (preferable to ibuprofen TID — once-daily dosing, better compliance, less GI risk). If insufficient, consider a 7-10 day course of a methylprednisolone dose pack (Medrol Dosepak) for acute radicular inflammation.\n- **Neuropathic pain agent**: Gabapentin 300 mg at bedtime, titrate by 300 mg every 3-5 days to 300 mg TID (target 900-1800 mg/day divided TID). Alternative: pregabalin 75 mg BID, titrate to 150 mg BID. Duloxetine 30-60 mg daily is another option with dual mechanism.\n- **Muscle relaxant**: Cyclobenzaprine 5-10 mg at bedtime for paravertebral spasm — short course (2-4 weeks). Avoid prolonged use.\n- **Topical**: Lidocaine 5% patch over the trapezius/paracervical region for local pain relief without systemic effects.\n- **Regarding opioid request** (addressed in Domain E): NOT indicated as first-line for cervical radiculopathy. Multimodal approach is far more effective and safer.\n\n**2. Physical Therapy**:\n- **Manual therapy**: Cervical traction (intermittent mechanical traction, 10-15 lbs, 15 minutes) — evidence supports traction for radiculopathy with foraminal stenosis. Opens the neuroforamen and reduces nerve root compression.\n- **Therapeutic exercise**: Cervical retraction exercises (chin tucks), deep neck flexor strengthening, scapular stabilization, upper thoracic extension mobility.\n- **Nerve gliding exercises**: Median nerve and C6 nerve root glides to reduce adhesions and improve neural mobility.\n- **Posture retraining**: Correct forward head posture and rounded shoulders. Ergonomic education.\n- **Modalities**: Heat, TENS for pain relief. Avoid cervical manipulation (thrust techniques) in the setting of disc herniation.\n- **Frequency**: 2-3 times per week for 6-8 weeks.\n\n**3. Activity Modifications**:\n- Avoid prolonged neck extension and ipsilateral rotation.\n- Cervical pillow for sleep (maintain neutral alignment).\n- Frequent position changes during computer work (every 30-45 minutes).\n\n**Epidural Steroid Injection (ESI) Indications**:\n- Persistent moderate-severe radicular pain despite 4-6 weeks of conservative management\n- Pain limiting participation in physical therapy\n- Patient prefers to avoid or delay surgery\n- **Approach**: Cervical transforaminal ESI at C5-C6 under fluoroscopic guidance is most targeted. Alternative: interlaminar approach. Use non-particulate steroid (dexamethasone) for transforaminal approach to minimize risk of particulate embolism.\n- **Expected outcomes**: 60-70% of patients report significant short-term relief (4-12 weeks). May be repeated up to 3 times per year.\n- **Risks to discuss**: Rare but serious — epidural hematoma, spinal cord injury, infection, dural puncture. Transient steroid effects (flushing, glucose elevation, insomnia).\n\n**Surgical Referral Criteria (Urgent or Elective)**:\n\n**Urgent surgical referral**:\n- Progressive neurologic deficit (worsening weakness)\n- Myelopathy signs (gait disturbance, bilateral hand dysfunction, hyperreflexia, Babinski)\n- Bowel/bladder dysfunction\n\n**Elective surgical referral**:\n- Failure of 6-12 weeks of comprehensive conservative management\n- Persistent significant pain and functional impairment\n- MRI-confirmed structural pathology correlating with clinical findings\n- Patient preference after informed discussion\n\n**Surgical options**: Anterior cervical discectomy and fusion (ACDF) is the standard for single-level disc herniation. Cervical disc arthroplasty is an alternative that preserves motion. Posterior foraminotomy for primarily foraminal pathology.',
