    const Section(
      title: '+ What ergonomic modifications would you recommend, and how do you approach disability documentation?',
      content: '**Ergonomic Workplace Assessment and Modifications**:\n\n**Workstation Evaluation** (refer to occupational therapist or ergonomic consultant):\n1. **Monitor position**: Top of screen at eye level or slightly below. Distance approximately arm\'s length (20-26 inches). Position directly in front — avoid turning head to view monitor. Consider dual-monitor setup positioned symmetrically if using two screens.\n2. **Chair**: Adjustable height so feet are flat on floor, thighs parallel to ground. Lumbar support maintaining natural lordosis. Armrests at elbow height to reduce shoulder/trapezius strain.\n3. **Keyboard and mouse**: At elbow height, elbows at approximately 90 degrees. Wrists in neutral position. Consider ergonomic keyboard (split keyboard) and vertical mouse to reduce forearm pronation strain.\n4. **Document holder**: Positioned between keyboard and monitor to avoid repeated neck flexion/rotation when referencing papers.\n5. **Headset**: If frequent phone use, a headset eliminates neck cradling the phone.\n\n**Behavioral Modifications**:\n- **Micro-breaks**: Stand and move every 30-45 minutes. Set a timer. Perform cervical retraction exercises during breaks.\n- **20-20-20 rule**: Every 20 minutes, look at something 20 feet away for 20 seconds (reduces eye strain which contributes to forward head posture).\n- **Work hours**: 10-12 hour days are a significant contributing factor. Recommend reducing to 8 hours during active treatment. If not possible, ensure 2-3 extended breaks per day.\n- **Standing desk**: Alternating sit-stand desk allows position changes. Aim for 30-50% standing time.\n\n**Disability Documentation**:\n\n**Approach — Modified Duty First**:\n- Most patients with cervical radiculopathy can continue working with modifications rather than full disability leave.\n- Document specific restrictions: No lifting >10 lbs with right arm. Avoid sustained overhead work. Ergonomic workstation required. Limit continuous computer work to 45-minute intervals with breaks.\n\n**If Modified Duty is Insufficient**:\n- Short-term disability may be appropriate during acute flare or post-procedure (ESI or surgery).\n- Duration: Typically 1-2 weeks for ESI recovery. 4-6 weeks for ACDF surgery.\n- Documentation requirements: Objective findings (exam, MRI, EMG), functional limitations, specific job demands that cannot be met, expected duration of disability, treatment plan.\n\n**Return-to-Work Planning**:\n- Graduated return: Start at reduced hours (4-6 hours) and increase by 2 hours per week.\n- Ergonomic modifications must be in place BEFORE returning to full duties.\n- Follow-up work capacity assessment at 4 and 8 weeks after return.\n- If permanent restrictions are needed (rare with radiculopathy), vocational counseling for job modification or role change.',
