      content: 'I would approach this with empathy, clear boundaries, and a strong alternative plan:\n\n**1. Validate the pain experience**:\n\'I believe you when you say your pain is severe. Not sleeping for weeks, not being able to work comfortably — that is miserable, and I take it seriously. I want to get your pain under control, and I have a plan to do that.\'\n\n**2. Educate about why opioids are not the right choice here**:\n\'I understand the Percocet helped, and that makes sense — opioids are powerful pain relievers. But here is why they are not the right treatment for your condition: Cervical radiculopathy pain is primarily nerve pain — burning, shooting, electrical. Opioids are actually poor at treating nerve pain. They mask the sensation temporarily but do not address the underlying nerve inflammation. More importantly, opioids have significant risks — dependence can develop in as little as 5-7 days of regular use, they cause sedation that would impair your work as a software engineer, and they can actually make chronic pain worse over time through a process called opioid-induced hyperalgesia.\'\n\n**3. Present the multimodal alternative with confidence**:\n\'Here is what I want to do instead, and I am confident this will be more effective:\n- **Tonight**: I am starting you on gabapentin, which specifically targets nerve pain. It also helps with sleep — many patients notice improved sleep within the first few nights.\n- **This week**: I am prescribing a short course of an oral steroid (Medrol Dosepak) to reduce the inflammation around the compressed nerve root. Many patients get significant relief within 48-72 hours.\n- **For muscle spasm**: Cyclobenzaprine at bedtime will address the muscle tightness AND help you sleep.\n- **This month**: I am referring you for physical therapy with cervical traction, which physically opens the space where the nerve is being compressed.\n- **If needed**: A cervical epidural steroid injection can deliver anti-inflammatory medication directly to the compressed nerve root — this is the most targeted treatment available.\'\n\n**4. Address the borrowed medication directly but without judgment**:\n\'I appreciate you being honest with me about taking your friend\'s medication. I am not here to judge you — you were desperate for relief. However, I do need to let you know that taking someone else\'s prescription medication is unsafe because it was not prescribed for you, your medical history, or your other medications. Going forward, I need you to take only medications that I or your other doctors prescribe.\'\n\n**5. Set expectations and follow-up**:\n\'I want to see you back in 2 weeks so we can assess how the gabapentin and other treatments are working. If we are not getting adequate pain control, I will escalate the treatment — but with targeted interventions, not with opioids. My goal is to treat the cause of your pain, not just cover it up. Does this plan make sense to you?\'\n\n**6. If the patient continues to insist**:\n\'I hear your frustration, and I respect that you are asking for what you think will help. But prescribing opioids for this condition would not be good medicine — it would be doing you a disservice. I would rather give you treatments that actually work for nerve pain than prescribe something that provides temporary relief but creates new problems. Let us give this plan 2 weeks, and if you are still in significant pain, we will adjust — but I will always be steering you toward the safest and most effective options.\'\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: Empathy without capitulation, evidence-based education, comprehensive multimodal alternative, non-judgmental response to borrowed medication, clear boundaries with maintained therapeutic alliance, specific follow-up plan.',
